s enough personal resonance  uh  now for instance when i um  for many years i was doing movies for  television  and i i prided myself on  i wrote them and directed them and i i  chose very carefully what i would do i  never took a movie for the money or  because i you know i was very careful  about what i what i chose if it was a  book i want they wanted me to adapt or  oftentimes they would come to me with a  failed script  and this you know they said look we  still want to make there's an idea here  we love we want to make this movie  the script didn't work can you do  something with the script and i would  take it over and  but in any project that came to me  the first thing i had to know  was  what's my emotional connection to this  can i find one  and if i can't find it i don't want to  do it because now i can't really i don't  feel like i can make it interesting and  a case in point  is i did a movie uh back she must be 98  1988  for tnt at the time called the hunley  and it was about the first uh submarine  used uh in warfare during the civil war  fascinating story um just fascinating  but uh you know i wasn't so much into a  war movie which it was it was i wanted  to make more of a character study but i  couldn't figure out what was driving  this main character to get into this  death trap this tube  that they all had to hand crank to go  forward once they dived  you know what could this be  and i suddenly had this idea  that he was in love with a woman  um who he left to go fight the in the  war  and she died while he was gone  and she drowned  and he's now obsessed with being  reunited with her  and that's sort of his death wish now  about going into the submarine and he  feels like this is his  his  destiny in a way  and it's never spoken i never told the  executives about it god forbid because  they would think you know i'm making  some whole of the movie  but that now allowed me to inform  all the writing and this and this  character's drive through the movie  with this kind of other thing  that we revealed in just little pieces  here and there a little flashback here  or a little thing here and suddenly by  the end of the movie you start to  realize of course this is why this guy  is like this and he's driving so hard  and so that's just an example of you  know how you can take a story that's  outside yourself  um but find a personal connection that  that allows you to really  lose yourself in that story and and make  it more than what appears just you know  on the surface  how do you know when you have a movie  idea as opposed to a television idea i  don't think there's any difference now  you know that we  that that's a question i could have  answered differently back in the 80s or  90s even  now there's just no  there's there's really no difference  because of streaming  um  you know i think in fact uh i think  there's far more  more interesting work being done  uh on the streaming services than in  future films uh you know i think feature  films right now the state of the  business is  ten poll franchise gigantic movies which  seem to be the only thing people are  willing to leave their homes for  um and i think that the  you know sort of like the directors you  you think about you know mark rydell and  cindy lumet and alan pakula  those directors  would be working for the streamers today  because their movies would never be made  and no one wants to go see um  you know uh kramer vs kramer they're not  gonna they're watching it on netflix  so i mean it's sad uh of course there's  nothing like the movie experience  but i'm so grateful that the streamers  are there because they don't have to get  unlike a broadcast network you know they  got to get 15 million people to watch a  show to make it worth their while  and the streamers can be in a much  different niche and they don't have to  they've got the subscriber base  and so they can appeal they can afford  to appeal to a lot of different groups  and so um  uh  honestly most of the ideas that i get  i don't think about them as movies i  think about them as something that might  be on amazon or apple plus or netflix  not that i don't again just loves going  to the movies because i'm addicted  i mean i easily see  five or six movies a week  probably two or three in the theater  uh and then the rest on  you know plus the television shows i'm  watching you know the great you know  better call saul you know you just can't  beat the stuff it's so well done  and in a way it's very gratifying  because i think that in the era that i  came of age in television  where  you had to  they pressure you to appeal to the  lowest common denominator  and we filmmakers always said but but  you don't have to do that you know trust  the audience a little bit  and now i think that's coming to  fruition because we are now  um in the television world or the  streaming world  uh we're dealing with stories that  i would no one ever make for television  years ago would never even touch them  uh and there's an appetite for them  people show up for them they want to see  them and i think that you know again a  lot of the low brow stuff now is in the  movie theater  not not on tv  why do purpose and passion matter when  choosing a screenplay  well in a lot of different ways in the  most practical way  um  making a movie  and when i say movie i'll you know it  applies to anything you know tv movie tv  show whatever just a movie  but uh it is difficult it's hard  it's hard to get it made um if you're  lucky enough to get it made they're hard  to make  um it's it's a very very  uh difficult undertaking to make a movie  it's the greatest thing in the world  but it's hard so you must have  a high level of purpose and passion to  bring you through this because you're  gonna get  uh stepped on you're gonna you know it's  a  you're gonna you're gonna lose your  money you'll get the money back you're  gonna  here comes the rain oh no i have a good  there's so 